From 681961317d9a27cd2f69751e763e2d7a73abb3b3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: HYDRA session <hydra@example.invalid>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 19:02:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Session 179: TT-A v2 -- register personality over SPI, margin
 nibble fix

v1 reaches the MOM through 24 pins and ties off everything it cannot fit:
the parameter ROM write port ('retuning is a v2 feature'), disp_accept, the
fence, and the calibration controls. Those tie-offs remove the two claims
that most need silicon evidence -- that the policy can be retuned after
tapeout, and that the calibration loop converges against real latencies.

v2 keeps v1 exactly and adds a second personality, selected by a strap
sampled while reset is low (ui_in[7:4]=0xA). LEGACY is v1, pin for pin:
all eight v1 tests pass unmodified, and tb_v1_v2_diff compares v2 against
the v1 RTL over 400,000 cycles and 3,118 random resets with no difference.
REGISTER exposes all of mom_top through a 14-register SPI map.

Measured with the register map: retuning the TPU row moves an 8x8x8 GEMM to
SIMD and PARAM_LOCK makes the same write inert; the calibration loop drives
the margin from 136 to 1 over 18 slow completions and then changes the
decision. Real margins are 32 and 145.

Also fixes the margin nibble. v1 saturated on obs_margin[31:12] and
otherwise output [15:12], a subset of the bits it had just tested, so it
could only ever read 0 or F -- both session 144 runs logged 0. Legacy now
saturates on [31:16] as its comment intended; the register personality uses
1 + floor(log2(margin)) and reports the full 32-bit value in RESULT.

New: hydra_tt_spi.sv (oversampled SPI target, formally proved, 6/6
mutations killed), hydra_tt_regs.sv, test/test_regs.py (9 tests),
test/mutate_sim.py (15 sabotages, 14 caught by the test named for each; the
survivor is documented as unobservable at the pins).

NOT DONE: the tile has not been hardened, so v2's area at tiles: 3x4 is
unknown. Harden before submitting, and raise tiles in info.yaml rather than
editing the floorplan by hand.
